<!--
  PHP2Shell (CVE-2026-109618 / CVE-2026-109619) execution detection for Wazuh.
  Tested on Wazuh 4.14.7 against PHP 8.5.11 with Apache mod_php.

  SIGNAL
  A shell execve by a non-root account. The vulnerable worker runs as the web
  service account (uid 33, www-data, on Debian), so an attacker-induced shell
  is non-root. Root shells are the machine's own housekeeping.

  WHY THE uid CONDITION IS LOAD-BEARING
  Without it this rule is unusable. On an idle test host with no attack
  running, the version without the uid field fired 40 times in a few minutes:
  every one uid 0, exe /usr/bin/dash, a0 "sh" or "/bin/sh", mostly a single
  ppid. Cron, systemd and the Wazuh agent's own wodles run "sh -c" constantly.
  At level 12 that buries the one event worth catching.

  The negative lookahead is used rather than matching uid 33, so the rule keeps
  working where the vulnerable service runs as another non-root account.

  REQUIRES
  auditd on the monitored host with execve recorded and shipped to the manager.
  An execve-only audit policy is enough. The rule reads audit.execve.a0 and
  audit.uid, and 80700 is the stock auditd parent.

  TUNING
  Change the id if 102007 is taken in your ruleset. On a host where a non-root
  interactive shell is normal, restrict the rule to your single-purpose web
  hosts with a <location> or a group condition. Note that an agent event's
  location is "(agent-name) any->/path", parentheses included, so a pattern
  written against a bare agent name will never match.
-->
<group name="audit,audit_command,">

  <rule id="102007" level="12">
    <if_sid>80700</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.execve.a0" type="pcre2">^((/usr)?/bin/)?(sh|bash|dash)$</field>
    <field name="audit.uid" type="pcre2">^(?!0$)[0-9]+$</field>
    <description>Web service account spawned a shell (PHP2Shell execution)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
